      "id": 1,
      "text": "Stafford began as Happy Valley, with its first school called Happy Valley (Lutwyche) State School before becoming Stafford State School in 1886.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stafford%2C_Queensland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "When the post office and a Collier Street school opened in 1886, they were named Stafford, and Happy Valley Road was renamed Stafford Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.queenslandplaces.com.au/stafford",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Stafford lies beside Kedron Brook and hosts Sparkes Hill, whose reservoir holds 94 megalitres—about 16% of Brisbane's water supply.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stafford%2C_Queensland",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "From 1940 to 1968, Stafford was served by electric trams, the last entirely new tram line built in Brisbane.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stafford%2C_Queensland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Chinese market gardens first appeared in Stafford in 1857, marking the suburb’s early agricultural diversity.",
